-- chem/magnetic.cub — Thiemann's magnetic operator at a vertex (QSD V §3.1, eqs. 3.9–3.11) for U(1). It is
--   Ĥ^B_v ∝ Σ_{Δ,Δ′ at v} ε^{jkl} ε^{mnp} Σ_i tr(τ_i A_l) tr(τ_i A_p) · Σ_I tr(τ_I h_{α_jk}) tr(τ_I h_{α_mn}),
-- A_e = h_{s_e}[h_{s_e}⁻¹, √V] = √V − M_e (M_e = h_e √V h_e⁻¹, chem/electric.cub), α_jk = s_j ∘ a_jk ∘ s_k⁻¹ a loop out
-- along the segment s_j of edge j, across the arc a_jk joining the segments' ends, back along s_k.
--
-- The gravitational factor. With τ_i = −iσ_i/2, Σ_i (τ_i)_ab (τ_i)_cd = −¼(2δ_ad δ_bc − δ_ab δ_cd), so
--   K_lp = Σ_i tr(τ_i A_l) tr(τ_i A_p) = −½ tr(A_l A_p) + ¼ tr A_l tr A_p = −½ tr(M_l M_p) + ¼ tr M_l tr M_p,
-- the V and √V tr M terms cancelling between the two (tr(A_l A_p) is O_lp of chem/electric.cub, tr A = 2√V − tr M).
-- The order of the operator entries is that of the indices, as in O_lp: A_l's entries left of A_p's.
--
-- The U(1) factor. For SU(N), tr(τ_I h) = tr(τ_I (h − h⁻¹))/2 is the Lie-algebra part of h, the curvature to first
-- order. U(1) is not semisimple: in its one-dimensional representation τ = i (tr ττ = −1) and tr(τ · 1) = i ≠ 0, so
-- the curvature is carried by the Lie-algebra part itself, tr(τ (h − h⁻¹)/2) = −(h − h⁻¹)/(2i) = −sin θ_α. On a
-- charge network h_α^{±1} adds ±1 along α, so the U(1) factor of two loops is
--   S_α S_β = −¼ (h_α h_β − h_α h_β⁻¹ − h_α⁻¹ h_β + h_α⁻¹ h_β⁻¹),
-- and for one loop twice, −¼ h_α² + ½ − ¼ h_α⁻².
--
-- Where the loops act. The segment s_j runs from v along edge j to the first point the operator creates, and the
-- triangulation is adapted to the graph, so s_j holds no vertex of it: every new arc is attached nearer v than every
-- arc already there. An edge's end at v is therefore its stack of segment labels from v outward, the last being the
-- label of the edge beyond every arc (shared with the edge's other end), and multiplying by α_jk^{±1} pushes a new
-- innermost segment on j (label + s) and on k (label − s, the loop returning along k). The electric operator reads
-- only the innermost labels (X_e on s_e). The arcs' ends are points of an edge where its two halves are collinear, so
-- every ε(I,J,K) there is 0, the volume is 0, and neither operator acts there.
import "electric.cub"

